From: Tyler Baker <tyler@foundries.io>
Date: Thu, 21 May 2026 16:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glib-unix: fall back to safe_fdwalk when close_range
 returns EPERM

g_closefrom() and g_fdwalk_set_cloexec() call close_range() and only
fall back to the per-fd safe_fdwalk loop when close_range() returns
ENOSYS (and EINVAL for the CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC variant). Container
seccomp profiles that block close_range typically return EPERM, not
ENOSYS, so the fallback never fires and every g_spawn user
(glib-compile-resources, dbus-binding-tool, gdbus-codegen,
g-ir-scanner, ...) breaks with:

    Failed to close file descriptor for child process
    (Operation not permitted)

Add EPERM to the set of errnos that trigger the fallback so glib works
inside restricted containers without needing the host's seccomp profile
loosened.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [workaround for restricted container
 seccomp; the right fix is to relax the container profile to allow
 close_range, but this patch keeps glib working until that lands]

Signed-off-by: Tyler Baker <tyler@foundries.io>
--- a/glib/glib-unix.c	2026-05-21 16:48:15.360343109 -0400
+++ b/glib/glib-unix.c	2026-05-21 16:48:15.368343152 -0400
@@ -850,7 +850,7 @@
    * fall back to safe_fdwalk(). Handle EINVAL in case `CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC`
    * is not supported. */
   ret = close_range (lowfd, G_MAXUINT, CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC);
-  if (ret == 0 || !(errno == ENOSYS || errno == EINVAL))
+  if (ret == 0 || !(errno == ENOSYS || errno == EINVAL || errno == EPERM))
     return ret;
 #endif  /* HAVE_CLOSE_RANGE */
 
@@ -897,7 +897,7 @@
    * Handle ENOSYS in case it’s supported in libc but not the kernel; if so,
    * fall back to safe_fdwalk(). */
   ret = close_range (lowfd, G_MAXUINT, 0);
-  if (ret == 0 || errno != ENOSYS)
+  if (ret == 0 || (errno != ENOSYS && errno != EPERM))
     return ret;
 #endif  /* HAVE_CLOSE_RANGE */
 
